**Read:** [Large Volume Inorganic Chemicals — Ammonia, Acids and Fertilisers, BREF LVIC-AAF](../sources/Ammonia-Acids-Fertilisers-BREF.lingua#r_45NWBJYAMXXSRV071A57EM02GB), the entire phosphoric-acid chapter, separation, purification and residual context; [Inorganic Chemical Technology — Nanda Kishore, NPTEL](../sources/Inorganic-Chemical-Technology-Kishore.lingua#r_77Z8T5RFYFNNXZ7XKZFKPRKDKR), the complete phosphoric-acid production teaching; [Chemical-industry process descriptions and emission factors — EPA AP-42](../sources/Chemical-Industry-Emission-Factors-EPA.lingua#r_16XA95GZSY1VD7GZNTSXWVNBC3), the whole phosphoric-acid section and original background evidence.

Wet-process phosphoric-acid manufacture couples phosphate-mineral reaction with acid, calcium-sulfate phase formation, solid/liquid separation and further purification or concentration. A thermally derived phosphorus route has different chemistry, utilities, product impurities and residuals. Compare complete routes rather than treating acid identity as a complete process description.

For a deliberately simplified hydroxyapatite account, Ca₅(PO₄)₃OH+5H₂SO₄→3H₃PO₄+5CaSO₄+H₂O is balanced. Actual mineral substitutions, fluoride, calcium-sulfate hydration, mother liquor, dissolved impurities and side reactions require their own species/water accounts. The symbolic equation is not an operating recipe or a prediction of the actual phases present.

Filtration/washing, acid recovery, impurity distribution and residue behavior connect reaction conditions with useful product and environmental work. A food, industrial or fertiliser use can require a different purification and evidence chain even at similar nominal acid concentration.

**Work:** reconstruct a complete public route with actual phase and impurity evidence, independently closing phosphorus, calcium, sulfur, water and one consequential trace constituent. Compare two reaction/separation/purification arrangements at the same product requirement. Explain uncertainty, retained liquor and the observations needed to test the proposed phase and recovery model.

